        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n25:en",
        "text": "Inductance affects the size of an alternating current. Figure 40 shows the textbook demonstration: a coil with substantial inductance and an incandescent lamp are connected in series, and a double-pole double-throw switch selects a DC or AC source.\nFor the comparison, the DC voltage equals the RMS AC voltage, and the DC switching transient is allowed to end.\nIn this circuit with resistance and inductance, the lamp is brighter under DC, indicating that the RMS AC current is smaller than the steady DC current.\nThis is an interpretation of a textbook demonstration, not an instruction to wire or measure a lighting circuit.\n\nThe explanation is self-induction. A changing current in the coil produces a changing magnetic field and an induced electric field that opposes the change in current.\nAfter a real coil is connected to a DC circuit, the current rises gradually rather than immediately reaching its steady value.\nWith AC, the voltage and current keep changing, so self-induction keeps affecting the current.\nFor the same source voltage, frequency, and other circuit conditions, a larger inductance $L$ gives a smaller AC current amplitude.\n\nFigure 40\n\nFigure 41\n\nWe now use an ideal-inductor model for the coil in Figure 41, whose resistance is negligible, to find its current.\nFirst relate the voltage across the coil to its self-induced electromotive force. A real coil still has resistance; this ideal model is not a wiring experiment.\n\nIn the ideal model of a wire with zero resistance, the total electric field along the wire inside it is zero.\nThis does not mean that the voltage across the coil is zero. The changing magnetic field produces an induced electric field $\\vec{E}_i$, while charges on the source terminals and wires produce a Coulomb field $\\vec{E}_k$.\nIn this ideal model, their effects along the wire are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, so their total effect cancels.\n\nFrom $\\vec{E}_i=-\\vec{E}_k$, moving a unit positive charge along the same direction gives work of equal magnitude and opposite sign for the two fields.\nThe induced field's work corresponds to the self-induced electromotive force $e_i$, and the Coulomb field's work corresponds to the voltage across the coil.\nWith the consistent direction convention used here:\n$e_i=-u$\n\nSuppose the current varies harmonically and is written using its amplitude:\n$i=I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega t$\nHere t is time, and the angular frequency describes how rapidly the current varies.\n\nThe self-induced electromotive force opposes the change in current. The prime denotes the rate of change of current with time:\n$e_i=-Li'=L\\omega I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\sin\\omega t$ (2.29)\n\nSince $u=-e_i$, the voltage across the coil is:\n$u=-L\\omega I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\sin\\omega t=L\\omega I_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\left(\\omega t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n$=U_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\left(\\omega t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ (2.30)\nThe second line continues the same equality. The sine and cosine forms describe the same voltage.\n\nHere $U_{\\mathrm{m}}=L\\omega I_{\\mathrm{m}}$ is the voltage amplitude.\nIt describes the magnitude of the peak voltage, not the voltage at every instant and not the RMS voltage.\n\n**The voltage across an ideal coil leads its current by** $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nEquivalently, **the current lags behind the voltage by** $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. This phase difference is a quarter of a cycle.\n\nFigure 42 shows this phase relationship.\nWhenever the coil's voltage reaches a positive or negative peak, the current is zero.\n\nFigure 42\n\nWhen the voltage is zero, the magnitude of the current is greatest.\nDo not confuse this separation of zero crossings and peaks with the in-phase current and voltage of a pure resistor.\n\nThe relationship between the voltage and current amplitudes gives:\n$I_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\frac{U_{\\mathrm{m}}}{\\omega L}$ (2.31)\nThis expression is for sinusoidal AC with nonzero angular frequency.\n\nWrite the product of angular frequency and inductance as the inductive reactance:\n$\\omega L=X_L$ (2.32)\nBoth current and voltage are sinusoidal at the same frequency. Taking their RMS values over a complete cycle gives:\n$I=\\frac{U}{X_L}$ (2.33)\n\n**Inductive reactance $X_L$ is the product of angular frequency and inductance.**\nIt has the same unit as resistance, but describes the effect of inductance on changing AC current, not resistance that continually converts electrical energy into heat.\n\nEquation (2.33) has a form similar to Ohm's law for DC, but the current and voltage here are RMS values.\nIt does not say that the instantaneous values are always in the same ratio: in an ideal inductor, current and voltage differ in phase by a quarter of a cycle.\n\n**Inductive reactance depends on frequency.** In steady DC, the current no longer changes, so the coil no longer produces a self-induced electromotive force opposing a change in current.\nAt $\\omega=0$, **the inductive reactance is $X_L=0$**. The steady DC current in a real circuit is still limited by resistance; dividing a DC voltage by zero is not a way to find it.\nFor a sinusoidal source with the same voltage amplitude, with inductance and other conditions fixed, a higher frequency gives greater inductive reactance and **a smaller current amplitude**."
      }
